AB  - Improving agricultural output and optimizing irrigation methods depend on precise and timely crop moisture monitoring. The goal of this project is to develop a remote crop moisture monitoring system that overcomes the drawbacks of existing methods and provides a more effective and scalable alternative. To collect thorough data on soil conditions in real time, the system integrates a range of sensor technologies, such as temperature, humidity, and soil moisture sensors. Wireless transmission of data from these sensors is made to a central platform for analysis and viewing. The ESP8266 module facilitates wireless communication and guarantees dependable data transfer over extended distances. The central platform processes the incoming data using modern data analytics and machine learning techniques, providing farmers with predictive analytics and useful insights. With the use of insights, farmers can schedule irrigation more effectively, use water more efficiently, and avoid over- or underwatering their crops. Farmers can monitor their fields remotely thanks to the system's user-friendly interface, which offers real-time warnings and comprehensive reports that can be accessed via PCs or mobile devices. The ultimate objective of this research is to enhance sustainable agriculture by optimizing agricultural output and water resource utilization. This system seeks to increase crop yields, decrease water waste, and contribute to the overall sustainability of farming methods by offering a reliable and effective crop moisture monitoring solution. © The Institution of Engineering & Technology 2024.
