TI  - Applications of Smart Agriculture in Irrigation Water Management in Developing Countries: The Way Forward
T2  - Climate-Smart and Resilient Food Systems and Security
LA  - English
KW  - Climate change; Crop water requirement; Internet of things; Precision farming; Remote sensing; Smart irrigation; Agricultural economics; Agricultural pollution; Agronomy; Climate change; Crops; Developing countries; Economics; Irrigation; Precision agriculture; Smart agriculture; Soil moisture; Sustainable development; Agricultural productivity; Crop water requirements; Food production; Irrigation water management; Precision-farming; Primary sources; Remote-sensing; Smart agricultures; Smart irrigation; World population; Remote sensing
AB  - Agriculture is the backbone of the world's population, being the primary source of food production. Climate change affects agriculture in various ways, including changes in typical temperatures, precipitation, and atmospheric condi-tions. Advanced automation of agronomic activities for land preparation, irrigation water management, pesticide application, fertilizer application and harvesting has substantially improved agricultural productivity. In many agricultural regions, deter-mining the exact amount of water to provide for many crops is an unsolved challenge due to the impact of climate change. One of the best solutions for overcoming this difficulty could be to use precision farming technologies to increase agricultural productivity, reduce pollution, and lower production costs while overcoming climate change issues. Smart agriculture plays an important role in agriculture to maintain the sustainability of agricultural systems and improve the country's economic growth. It means that smart farming supports agronomic practices such as estimating crop area, monitoring crop growth, estimating soil moisture, evaluating soil fertility, detecting crop stress, detecting diseases and pests, yield estimation, and weather forecasting. The smart irrigation system is being used in agriculture to address water scarcity due to climate change. This review covers the importance of smart farming applications in irrigation water management to increase farm productivity by overcoming climate change, using less human energy, increasing efficiency, and improving management techniques through precision farming technologies. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2024.
